.SH "JOUET GRAPH EXPLORE"
Find the code that answers a question, with its source and neighbours

Ranks symbols by how well their names (split into words), qualified names, docstrings and signatures match the question, puts tests after the code they test unless the question is about tests, and prints each with its signature, docstring, callers, callees and the start of its source. The answer is paged rather than printed whole: a dense answer stays in the agent's context for the rest of the session.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet graph explore [OPTIONS] [<QUERY>] ...
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-budget\fR \fI<BYTES>\fR
The most bytes to print (default 6000; 0 is no limit)
.TP
\fB\-\-source\-budget\fR \fI<BYTES>\fR
The most bytes of one symbol's source (default 1200; 0 for none)
.TP
\fB\-\-page\fR \fI<N>\fR
Which page of a longer answer to print (default 1)
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<QUERY>\fR
The question, or the names you know (`retry backoff for webhooks`)
.SH "JOUET GRAPH AFFECTED"
List the test files that depend on changed files

Follows the graph backwards from each FILE, through imports, calls, references and inheritance, to every test file that reaches it, and shows the chain that does. A FILE the graph cannot speak for (not indexed, or in a language jouet does not extract) is listed as unknown, and the exit code is 1: run the whole suite. A fallback for languages without a native tool; `jouet test \-\-affected` prefers the native one.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet graph affected [OPTIONS] [<FILES>] ...
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<FILES>\fR
The changed files
.SH "JOUET GRAPH PRUNE"
Delete the graphs of worktrees that no longer exist

Each worktree's graph records its root. This deletes every graph in $XDG_CACHE_HOME/jouet/graph whose root is gone, and keeps one whose root exists or cannot be checked. Querying a graph also does this, at most once a day.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet graph prune [OPTIONS]
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
.SH "JOUET HANDOFF"
Record the facts about this checkout for the next session

Gathers the branch, its upstream and ahead/behind counts, HEAD, an operation in progress, uncommitted and untracked files, the branch's PR and its `pr status` attention codes, the session's last test, lint and build runs (recorded by `jouet setup`'s hooks), background processes running in the worktree (Linux), and its `jouet job`s. Writes them as markdown with the data embedded, to `<git dir>/jouet/handoff.md` unless \-o names a file (`.json` writes JSON; `\-` is stdout). Facts only: what the work is and what to do next is the agent's to add.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet handoff [OPTIONS]
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-o, \-\-output\fR \fI<FILE>\fR
Write the handoff to FILE (`\-` is stdout)
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-pr\fR
Do not look up the branch's PR on GitHub
.TP
\fB\-\-session\fR \fI<ID>\fR
The agent session whose check runs to include (default JOUET_SESSION_ID, else CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID)
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
.SH "JOUET PICKUP"
Print a handoff and check that it still holds

Reads `<git dir>/jouet/handoff.md`, or FILE, prints it, and compares it with the checkout: the branch, HEAD, the upstream, an operation in progress, uncommitted and untracked files, and whether its background processes still run. Exits 0 when nothing has drifted and 1 when something has. The PR is not fetched again: run `jouet pr status` for its current state.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet pickup [OPTIONS] [<FILE>]
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<FILE>\fR
The handoff to read (`\-` is stdin)
.SH "JOUET STATS"
Show what jouet saved: measured from its ledger, estimated from your history

Counts what the hook ledger recorded (jouet command runs, rule decisions and what the agent did after a denial, checks, and failing CI logs returned as excerpts), then estimates the input tokens those runs saved against your own Claude Code and Codex history from before `jouet setup`. Every estimate shows its basis, and a kind of work with fewer than 20 runs before setup is not estimated. Without a ledger, it shows what one run of each command would save.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet stats [OPTIONS]
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-since\fR \fI<TIME>\fR
Only ledger entries since TIME: a duration ago such as 7d or 12h, a date such as 2026\-10\-01, or RFC 3339
.TP
\fB\-\-harness\fR \fI<HARNESS>\fR
Which harness: claude, codex or all (default all)
.TP
\fB\-\-session\fR \fI<ID>\fR
Only one session's ledger entries
.TP
\fB\-\-summary\fR
Print one line about what jouet saved, or nothing, for a stop hook. `[stats] summary` in the config file or JOUET_STATS_SUMMARY sets its style: quips (default), plain or off
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
.SH "JOUET PR"
Inspect pull requests
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet pr [OPTIONS] <COMMAND>
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
.SH "JOUET PR STATUS"
Show where PRs stand and why any need attention

Prints one line per PR, then a summary. All PRs are fetched in one batched GraphQL query. Each PR lists fixed attention codes: conflict, checks_failing, checks_missing, changes_requested, unresolved_threads, behind_base, draft and mergeable_unknown.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet pr status [OPTIONS] [<PRS>] ...
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-mine\fR
Add the open PRs authored by the token's user
.TP
\fB\-\-search\fR \fI<QUERY>\fR
Add the PRs matching a GitHub search query
.TP
\fB\-\-repo\fR \fI<OWNER/NAME>\fR
The repository for bare numbers, branches, \-\-mine and \-\-search. Repeatable. Defaults to the git remote
.TP
\fB\-\-settle\fR \fI<DUR>\fR
How long to wait for GitHub to compute an unknown mergeable state (default 20s; 0 disables)
.TP
\fB\-\-fail\-on\-attention\fR
Exit 1 when any PR needs attention
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<PRS>\fR
PRs as numbers, URLs, OWNER/REPO#N or branch names. Defaults to the current branch's PR
.SH "JOUET PR NEXT"
Say the one thing each PR needs next, and the command for it

Works through a fixed order and stops at the first state that applies: merged, closed, draft, conflict, mergeable_unknown, behind_base, checks_failing, checks_pending, changes_requested, unresolved_threads, awaiting_approval, blocked, auto_merge, ready_to_merge. The states come from the same data and attention codes as `pr status`. Exits 0 when nothing is left to do, 10 when the agent must act and 20 when waiting on someone or something else; with several PRs, 10 wins over 20. These two codes are outside the shared table on purpose.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet pr next [OPTIONS] [<PRS>] ...
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-mine\fR
Add the open PRs authored by the token's user
.TP
\fB\-\-search\fR \fI<QUERY>\fR
Add the PRs matching a GitHub search query
.TP
\fB\-\-repo\fR \fI<OWNER/NAME>\fR
The repository for bare numbers, branches, \-\-mine and \-\-search. Repeatable. Defaults to the git remote
.TP
\fB\-\-settle\fR \fI<DUR>\fR
How long to wait for GitHub to compute an unknown mergeable state (default 20s; 0 disables)
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<PRS>\fR
PRs as numbers, URLs, OWNER/REPO#N or branch names. Defaults to the current branch's PR
.SH "JOUET PR LAND"
Merge a PR that is ready, honouring branch protection

Refuses (exit 4) unless `pr next` says ready_to_merge, and says what is missing. With \-\-update, a branch behind its base is first updated on GitHub by merging the base in (never a force\-push), and its new checks are waited for. The merge goes through the API with the head SHA as a guard, the way the merge button does, so branch protection applies and is never bypassed. Then the PR is confirmed merged and the merge commit printed. It asks first only when a person is at a terminal (stdin and stderr are terminals and no coding agent is detected); anyone else must pass \-\-yes or \-\-dry\-run, or it exits 2 before contacting GitHub.

While jouet's hooks keep a ledger, it also refuses (exit 4) unless they recorded a full test run passing at the PR head's tree; pass \-\-skip\-verify with a reason, which the ledger keeps, to land anyway.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet pr land [OPTIONS] [<PR>]
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-repo\fR \fI<OWNER/NAME>\fR
The repository for a bare number or branch. Defaults to the git remote
.TP
\fB\-\-method\fR \fI<METHOD>\fR
How to merge: squash, merge or rebase (default squash)
.TP
\fB\-\-update\fR
Update a branch that is behind its base by merging the base in on GitHub, then wait for its checks
.TP
\fB\-\-yes\fR
Merge without asking
.TP
\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR
Print the plan and change nothing
.TP
\fB\-\-timeout\fR \fI<DUR>\fR
How long to wait for checks after \-\-update (default 30m)
.TP
\fB\-\-interval\fR \fI<DUR>\fR
Time between the first checks of that wait; backs off to 60s (default 10s)
.TP
\fB\-\-settle\fR \fI<DUR>\fR
How long to wait for GitHub to compute an unknown mergeable state (default 20s; 0 disables)
.TP
\fB\-\-skip\-verify\fR \fI<REASON>\fR
Land without a full test pass recorded at the PR head's tree, and say why; the reason is kept in the hook ledger
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<PR>\fR
The PR: a number, URL, OWNER/REPO#N or branch. Defaults to the current branch's PR
.SH "JOUET PR THREADS"
List, reply to and resolve review threads

Lists unresolved threads by default, outdated ones included and marked. Each thread is addressed by its first comment's ID, the number in the web UI's `#discussion_r` links. Bodies are condensed: HTML comments and badges are removed, `<details>` collapse to their summary and each comment is cut at 30 lines with a marker; suggestion blocks are always kept whole.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet pr threads [OPTIONS] [<PR>] [COMMAND]
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-repo\fR \fI<OWNER/NAME>\fR
The repository for a bare number or branch. Defaults to the git remote
.TP
\fB\-\-all\fR
List resolved threads too
.TP
\fB\-\-resolved\fR
List only resolved threads
.TP
\fB\-\-outdated\fR
List only outdated threads
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-outdated\fR
Leave out outdated threads
.TP
\fB\-\-author\fR \fI<LOGIN>\fR
Only threads started by LOGIN. Repeatable
.TP
\fB\-\-bots\fR
Only threads started by bots: logins ending in `[bot]`, and the config's `bots`
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-bots\fR
Leave out threads started by bots
.TP
\fB\-\-since\fR \fI<TIME>\fR
Only threads with a comment posted at or after TIME: RFC 3339, a duration ago such as 2h, or @SHA for when that commit was pushed. Goes by when comments were posted, never by the commit they are on
.TP
\fB\-\-path\fR \fI<GLOB>\fR
Only threads on paths matching GLOB, a directory, or (without a /) a file name. Repeatable
.TP
\fB\-\-include\-reviews\fR
Also list review summaries and PR conversation comments
.TP
\fB\-\-context\fR \fI<N>\fR
Show N lines of the file around each thread's line
.TP
\fB\-\-full\fR
Show comment bodies unaltered
.TP
\fB\-\-fail\-if\-unresolved\fR
Exit 1 when any listed thread is unresolved
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<PR>\fR
The PR: a number, URL, OWNER/REPO#N or branch. Defaults to the current branch's PR
.SH "JOUET PR THREADS REPLY"
Reply to a thread

`{sha}` in the reply becomes the short HEAD SHA, and is refused unless HEAD is on the PR's branch on GitHub. The configured reply footer is appended. A reply that is already the thread's last comment from you is not posted again.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet pr threads reply [OPTIONS] <ID> [<PR>]
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-body\fR \fI<TEXT>\fR
The reply
.TP
\fB\-\-body\-file\fR \fI<FILE>\fR
Read the reply from FILE (`\-` is stdin)
.TP
\fB\-\-resolve\fR
Resolve the thread after replying
.TP
\fB\-\-ignore\-resolved\fR
Skip a thread that is already resolved instead of failing
.TP
\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR
Check everything and show what would be posted, posting nothing
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<ID>\fR
The thread's ID
.TP
\fB<PR>\fR
The PR. Defaults to the current branch's PR
.SH "JOUET PR THREADS RESOLVE"
Resolve threads

Every ID is checked before any thread is resolved. A PR given after the IDs must not be a bare number: write it as #N.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet pr threads resolve [OPTIONS] [<ARGS>] ...
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-reply\fR \fI<TEXT>\fR
Reply with TEXT before resolving each thread
.TP
\fB\-\-ignore\-resolved\fR
Skip threads that are already resolved instead of failing
.TP
\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR
Check everything and show what would happen, changing nothing
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<ARGS>\fR
Thread IDs, then optionally the PR
.SH "JOUET PR THREADS UNRESOLVE"
Unresolve threads

A PR given after the IDs must not be a bare number: write it as #N.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet pr threads unresolve [OPTIONS] [<ARGS>] ...
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR
Check everything and show what would happen, changing nothing
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<ARGS>\fR
Thread IDs, then optionally the PR
.SH "JOUET PR THREADS APPLY"
Reply to and resolve many threads from a JSON list

The list holds {"id": N, "reply": "...", "resolve": true} entries. Every entry is checked before anything is posted. Re\-running the same list after a failure is safe: resolved threads and replies already posted are skipped.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet pr threads apply [OPTIONS] [<ARGS>] ...
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-file\fR \fI<FILE>\fR
Read the list from FILE (`\-` is stdin)
.TP
\fB\-\-ignore\-resolved\fR
Skip threads that are already resolved instead of failing
.TP
\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR
Check everything and show what would be posted, posting nothing
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<ARGS>\fR
`\-` to read the list from stdin, and optionally the PR
.SH "JOUET READ"
Read a file by its outline, a symbol or a range

Reading a large file whole to find one thing fills the context with code that is not needed. Read it in steps instead:

1. `\-\-outline`: every definition with its line range, as `kind name
   (start\-end)`, the name qualified by its scope (`Vfox::download`).
2. `\-\-symbol NAME`: one definition's code. NAME is a name, a kind and a
   name (`fn download`, `class Cart`), or a qualified name
   (`Vfox::download`, `Shop.Cart`); every definition it matches is printed.
3. `\-\-range A:B`: lines A to B (`A:` to the end, `:B` from the start).

With none of them, a file of up to 300 lines is printed whole, and a longer one as its outline. Code is printed with line numbers, at most \-\-max\-lines lines (default 400; 0 is no limit) with a marker for the rest. Inside an agent session, lines this session was already shown, from a file unchanged since, collapse to one line; \-\-again prints them. Definitions come from tree\-sitter, for Rust, Python, JavaScript, TypeScript, Go, Ruby, Java, C and C++.

Exits 3 for a file or symbol that does not exist, and 2 for an outline or symbol of a file whose language jouet cannot read.

Examples:
  jouet read src/backend/vfox.rs \-\-outline
  jouet read src/backend/vfox.rs \-\-symbol 'Vfox::download'
  jouet read src/backend/vfox.rs \-\-range 120:160
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet read [OPTIONS] <PATH>
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-outline\fR
List the file's definitions and their line ranges
.TP
\fB\-\-symbol\fR \fI<NAME>\fR
Print the definitions this names: NAME, KIND NAME or Scope::NAME
.TP
\fB\-\-range\fR \fI<A:B>\fR
Print lines A to B: `A:B`, `A:` or `:B`
.TP
\fB\-\-max\-lines\fR \fI<N>\fR
The most lines of code to print (default 400; 0 is no limit)
.TP
\fB\-\-again\fR
Print lines this session was already shown, too
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<PATH>\fR
The file to read
.SH "JOUET REVIEW-PACKAGE"
Write one file for reviewing a range of commits

The file holds the commits, oldest first, a diffstat, and the full diff with \-\-context lines around each change. Refuses an empty range, and a BASE that is not an ancestor of HEAD (exit 4), since the diff would then show changes HEAD does not make. Written to `<git dir>/jouet/review\-BASE\-HEAD.md` unless \-o names a file (`\-` is stdout).
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet review\-package [OPTIONS] <BASE> [<HEAD>]
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-o, \-\-output\fR \fI<FILE>\fR
Write the package to FILE (`\-` is stdout)
.TP
\fB\-\-context\fR \fI<N>\fR
Lines of context around each change (default 10)
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<BASE>\fR
The commit the review starts after, such as origin/main
.TP
\fB<HEAD>\fR
The last commit to review (default HEAD)
.SH "JOUET SEARCH"
Search files like rg, within a budget, grouped by symbol

Embeds ripgrep's walker and searcher, so it respects .gitignore, skips hidden and binary files, and is about as fast as rg. Patterns are rg's regular expressions (\-F for a literal). Beyond rg:

\- Matches are grouped by file, then by the function, method, class or
  module they are in (for Rust, Python, JavaScript, TypeScript, Go,
  Ruby, Java, C and C++).
\- Output stops at a byte budget, in total and per file, and says what it
  left out: `... 4 more in src/x.rs`, and a closing line naming the files
  it never reached. Nothing is cut silently; \-\-budget 0 removes a limit.
\- Inside an agent session (JOUET_SESSION_ID, CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID or
  CODEX_THREAD_ID), a match this session was already shown, in a file
  that has not changed since, collapses to one line (`src/x.rs: 3 already
  shown, unchanged`). \-\-again shows them anyway.

With \-\-structural, PATTERN is code in \-\-lang, matched by ast\-grep: `$X` is one node, `$$$X` any number, and only files in that language are searched. Quote it in single quotes, or the shell expands `$X` (`jouet agent\-help edit` has more on patterns).

Exits 0 with matches, 1 with none, 2 for a bad pattern or glob, and 3 for a path that does not exist.

Examples:
  jouet search 'fn download' src/
  jouet search \-F 'client.get(' \-g '*.rs'
  jouet search \-i timeout \-\-budget 2000 \-\-file\-budget 400
  jouet search \-l TODO
  jouet search \-\-structural \-\-lang rust '$X.unwrap()'
  jouet search \-\-structural \-\-lang go 'if err != nil { return $$$ }'
  jouet search \-\-structural \-\-lang typescript 'useEffect($FN, [])'
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet search [OPTIONS] <PATTERN> [<PATHS>] ...
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-F, \-\-fixed\-strings\fR
Treat the pattern as a literal string
.TP
\fB\-i, \-\-ignore\-case\fR
Match case\-insensitively
.TP
\fB\-w, \-\-word\-regexp\fR
Match whole words only
.TP
\fB\-g, \-\-glob\fR \fI<GLOB>\fR
Include or exclude files, as rg's \-g: `*.rs`, `!*_test.go`. Repeatable
.TP
\fB\-\-hidden\fR
Search hidden files and directories too
.TP
\fB\-l, \-\-files\-with\-matches\fR
List the matching files with their match counts, not the lines
.TP
\fB\-\-budget\fR \fI<BYTES>\fR
The most bytes to print in total (default 6000; 0 is no limit)
.TP
\fB\-\-file\-budget\fR \fI<BYTES>\fR
The most bytes to print from one file (default 1500; 0 is no limit)
.TP
\fB\-\-again\fR
Show matches this session was already shown, too
.TP
\fB\-\-structural\fR
Match PATTERN as code, with ast\-grep: `$X` is one node, `$$$X` any number. Needs \-\-lang
.TP
\fB\-\-lang\fR \fI<LANG>\fR
The language of a \-\-structural pattern: rust, python, javascript, typescript, tsx, go, ruby, java, c or cpp
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<PATTERN>\fR
The regular expression to search for (a literal with \-F)
.TP
\fB<PATHS>\fR
Files and directories to search (default .)
.SH "JOUET TEST"
Run the test suite, or only the tests a change affects

Without \-\-affected, runs the repository's test task as the full suite: a mise `test` task, a package.json `test` script, Cargo.toml (`cargo test \-\-workspace`), a justfile or Makefile `test` target or go.mod, else the whole suite of each test runner found. With \-\-affected, runs only the tests that the changes since the merge base with \-\-since (default: the upstream, else the default branch), uncommitted and untracked files included, affect. Adapters delegate to each runner's own selection: vitest's and jest's module graphs, pytest\-testmon when it is installed, else a static import graph. The whole suite runs instead when a lockfile, tool versions, CI, the test configuration, a conftest or setup file, or a file no adapter can trace changed; nothing runs for docs only. Each test is listed with the chain of imports that picked it, and the command. Affected runs count as partial: the full suite still has to pass before you finish or push. Words after `\-\-` go to the runner. Exits 1 when tests fail, 2 for a usage error (the runner's too), 4 when no runner is found or installed.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet test [OPTIONS] [<EXTRA>] ...
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-affected\fR
Run only the tests the changes affect
.TP
\fB\-\-since\fR \fI<REF>\fR
Measure changes from the merge base with REF (default: the upstream, else the default branch)
.TP
\fB\-\-adapter\fR \fI<NAME>\fR
Use only this adapter: vitest, jest, pytest or graph (Go, Ruby and Java, from the code graph)
.TP
\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR
Print what would run and why, running nothing
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<EXTRA>\fR
Arguments for the test runner, after `\-\-`
.SH "JOUET WAIT"
Block until a condition holds, then print one summary

Replaces `sleep N; check; repeat` loops. The first check is immediate; later ones back off from \-\-interval to 60s and speed up again when the state changes. Exits 0 when the condition holds and 124 on timeout, where running the same command again keeps waiting. In human mode a live status line is shown on stderr; otherwise each change of state is one line on stderr, so a backgrounded wait's log stays small.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet wait [OPTIONS] <COMMAND>
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-timeout\fR \fI<DUR>\fR
Give up after DUR, such as 90s, 10m or 1h30m (default 30m)
.TP
\fB\-\-interval\fR \fI<DUR>\fR
Time between the first checks; backs off to 60s (default 10s)
.TP
\fB\-\-confirm\-conflict\fR \fI<DUR>\fR
How long a conflict GitHub reports must persist before `wait ci` and `wait pr` believe it (default 10s). For tests
.TP
\fB\-\-jsonl\fR
Print one JSON event per line, then the result document
.TP
\fB\-\-quiet\fR
Print no progress and no text result, only the exit code (and JSON when asked for)
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
.SH "JOUET WAIT CI"
Wait for a PR's checks, and show why any failed

Follows the PR head, switching to a new head when one is pushed, unless \-\-sha pins a commit. Returns on the first failed check unless \-\-all. Skipped and neutral checks count as passing. A conflicted PR returns `conflict` (exit 4), since pull_request workflows will not run, once GitHub reports it again 10s later (right after a push it can still answer for the old head); no checks within \-\-appear\-timeout returns `no_checks` (exit 1). For each failed Actions job, the failed step's log is excerpted and the full log saved. Waits on the same PR share their state, so they poll GitHub once.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet wait ci [OPTIONS] [<PR>]
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-repo\fR \fI<OWNER/NAME>\fR
The repository for a bare number or branch. Defaults to the git remote
.TP
\fB\-\-sha\fR \fI<SHA>\fR
Wait on this commit's checks instead of following the PR head
.TP
\fB\-\-required\fR
Only checks that branch protection requires
.TP
\fB\-\-check\fR \fI<GLOB>\fR
Only checks whose name matches GLOB (`*` and `?`). Repeatable
.TP
\fB\-\-ignore\fR \fI<GLOB>\fR
Leave out checks whose name matches GLOB. Repeatable
.TP
\fB\-\-all\fR
Wait for every check to finish, even after one fails
.TP
\fB\-\-log\-lines\fR \fI<N>\fR
Lines of each failure log to show (default 60; 0 shows the whole failed step)
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-logs\fR
Do not fetch failure logs
.TP
\fB\-\-appear\-timeout\fR \fI<DUR>\fR
How long checks may take to appear on a commit (default 3m)
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<PR>\fR
The PR: a number, URL, OWNER/REPO#N or branch. Defaults to the current branch's PR
.SH "JOUET WAIT RUN"
Wait for a GitHub Actions run

Returns when the run completes, or on its first failed job, with the failed step's log excerpted as for `wait ci`.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet wait run [OPTIONS] <RUN>
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-repo\fR \fI<OWNER/NAME>\fR
The repository for a bare run ID. Defaults to the git remote
.TP
\fB\-\-log\-lines\fR \fI<N>\fR
Lines of each failure log to show (default 60; 0 shows the whole failed step)
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-logs\fR
Do not fetch failure logs
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<RUN>\fR
The run ID, or its Actions URL
.SH "JOUET WAIT PR"
Wait for a PR to become mergeable, merged, approved, ready, ...

\-\-until is one of: mergeable (keeps waiting while GitHub reports UNKNOWN; exit 1 on a conflict), merged (exit 1 if closed unmerged), closed, approved (exit 1 when changes are requested), checks\-registered (any check on the head), ready (no `pr status` attention codes and no pending checks; exit 1 on conflict, checks_failing, changes_requested or unresolved_threads), or any\-change (the next push, check result, review, thread or state change, printed as one event). A conflict counts once GitHub reports it again 10s later, since right after a push it can still answer for the old head.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet wait pr [OPTIONS] [<PR>]
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-repo\fR \fI<OWNER/NAME>\fR
The repository for a bare number or branch. Defaults to the git remote
.TP
\fB\-\-until\fR \fI<CONDITION>\fR
The condition to wait for
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<PR>\fR
The PR: a number, URL, OWNER/REPO#N or branch. Defaults to the current branch's PR
.SH "JOUET WAIT FILE"
Wait for a file to exist, contain a match, or be gone
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet wait file [OPTIONS] <PATH>
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-contains\fR \fI<REGEX>\fR
Wait until a line of the file matches REGEX, and print that line
.TP
\fB\-\-gone\fR
Wait until nothing exists at PATH
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<PATH>\fR
The file (or directory) to watch
.SH "JOUET WAIT LOG"
Wait for a line matching a regex to be appended to a log

Follows the path like `tail \-F`: a file that is rotated or truncated is read again from its start, and one that does not exist yet is waited for. Starts at the current end of the file and prints the matching line.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet wait log [OPTIONS] <PATH>
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-match\fR \fI<REGEX>\fR
The regex a line must match
.TP
\fB\-\-from\-start\fR
Also search what the file already holds
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<PATH>\fR
The log file to follow
.SH "JOUET WAIT PORT"
Wait for a TCP port to accept connections, or to stop
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet wait port [OPTIONS] <ADDR>
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-closed\fR
Wait until the port refuses connections instead
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<ADDR>\fR
HOST:PORT, or just PORT for localhost
.SH "JOUET WAIT HTTP"
Wait for a URL to answer with a status, and optionally a match

Any 2xx status counts unless \-\-status says otherwise. Redirects are followed, except when \-\-status asks for a 3xx.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet wait http [OPTIONS] <URL>
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-status\fR \fI<CODE>\fR
The status code to wait for (default: any 2xx)
.TP
\fB\-\-contains\fR \fI<REGEX>\fR
Also wait until a line of the body matches REGEX, and print that line
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<URL>\fR
The http:// or https:// URL to GET
.SH "JOUET WAIT PID"
Wait for a process to exit
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet wait pid [OPTIONS] <PID>
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<PID>\fR
The process ID
.SH "JOUET WAIT CMD"
Re\-run a command until it exits with the expected code

Runs CMD directly, not through a shell, every \-\-interval. A run still going at the timeout is stopped, though every run gets at least one \-\-interval, so `\-\-timeout 0` runs it once. Prints the last run's exit code and the tail of its output, and saves the full output to a log.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet wait cmd [OPTIONS] [<CMD>] ...
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-expect\-exit\fR \fI<N>\fR
The exit code to wait for (default 0)
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<CMD>\fR
The command and its arguments, after `\-\-`
.SH "JOUET WAIT JOB"
Wait for a `jouet job` to end, or to be ready

\-\-until exited (the default) is met when the job exits 0 or `jouet job stop` stopped it, and fails (exit 1) when it exits with another code. \-\-until ready is met while it runs and its ready check passes: \-\-port or \-\-http, else the one it was started with. A job that ends before it is ready fails. On a failure or a timeout, the end of its log is shown. Exits 3 when this work tree has no such job.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet wait job [OPTIONS] <ID>
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-until\fR \fI<CONDITION>\fR
`exited` (the default) or `ready`
.TP
\fB\-\-port\fR \fI<N>\fR
Ready once localhost's port N accepts connections
.TP
\fB\-\-http\fR \fI<URL>\fR
Ready once URL answers with a 2xx status
.TP
\fB\-\-log\-lines\fR \fI<N>\fR
Lines of its log to show on failure or timeout (default 60; 0 shows all of it)
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-logs\fR
Do not show its log
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<ID>\fR
The job's ID
.SH "JOUET WAIT DAEMON"
Wait for a pitchfork daemon to be ready, or to stop

For a project's long\-running services, the daemons pitchfork runs (one\-off work is `wait job`'s). Reads `pitchfork status ID \-\-json` in this directory, so a short ID resolves in this project's namespace. Ready is `running` and, when there is a port to try (\-\-port, or the port pitchfork says the daemon binds) or a URL (\-\-http), answering there: pitchfork's JSON does not say whether its own ready check passed. \-\-until stopped waits until the daemon no longer runs. A daemon that errors or fails ends the wait with exit 1; then, and on a timeout, the end of its log is shown and the whole log is saved. Exits 3 when pitchfork has no such daemon and 4 when pitchfork is not installed.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet wait daemon [OPTIONS] <ID>
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-until\fR \fI<CONDITION>\fR
`ready` (the default) or `stopped`
.TP
\fB\-\-port\fR \fI<N>\fR
Ready once localhost's port N accepts connections
.TP
\fB\-\-http\fR \fI<URL>\fR
Ready once URL answers with a 2xx status
.TP
\fB\-\-log\-lines\fR \fI<N>\fR
Lines of its log to show on failure or timeout (default 60; 0 shows all of it)
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-logs\fR
Do not read its log
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<ID>\fR
The daemon's ID: `api`, or `namespace/api`
.SH "JOUET JOB"
Run one\-off background work, such as a build or a server for one check

A job is a command running in the background, detached in a process group of its own, with its output in one log. Jobs belong to the work tree they were started in. No daemon runs: each job is a file under jouet's state directory. Unix only; on Windows, start and stop exit 4.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet job [OPTIONS] <COMMAND>
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
.SH "JOUET JOB START"
Start a command in the background and return once it is ready

Runs CMD (directly, not through a shell) in this directory, with stdin closed and stdout and stderr in the job's log. With \-\-port, \-\-http or \-\-output, waits up to \-\-timeout (default 60s) for the job to be ready and fails (exit 1) with the end of its log if it exits first; a job still not ready at the timeout keeps running (exit 124). Without one, it returns at once, unless the command fails straight away. Refuses (exit 4) while a job of the same ID runs, unless \-\-replace stops it first.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet job start [OPTIONS] <ID> [<CMD>] ...
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-port\fR \fI<N>\fR
Ready once localhost's port N accepts connections
.TP
\fB\-\-http\fR \fI<URL>\fR
Ready once URL answers with a 2xx status
.TP
\fB\-o, \-\-output\fR \fI<REGEX>\fR
Ready once a line of the job's log matches REGEX
.TP
\fB\-\-timeout\fR \fI<DUR>\fR
How long to wait for the ready check (default 60s)
.TP
\fB\-\-replace\fR
Stop a running job of the same ID first
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<ID>\fR
The job's ID: letters, digits, `_`, `.` and `\-`
.TP
\fB<CMD>\fR
The command and its arguments, after `\-\-`
.SH "JOUET JOB LS"
List this work tree's jobs: running, exited, stopped or gone

`gone` is a job that is no longer running with nothing recorded about how it ended. Jobs that ended more than a week ago are removed.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet job ls [OPTIONS]
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
.SH "JOUET JOB LOGS"
Print the end of a job's log
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet job logs [OPTIONS] <ID>
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-n, \-\-lines\fR \fI<N>\fR
Lines to print (default 50; 0 prints all of it)
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<ID>\fR
The job's ID
.SH "JOUET JOB STOP"
Stop jobs: SIGTERM to the whole process group, then SIGKILL

A job that has already ended is left as it is.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet job stop [OPTIONS] [<IDS>] ...
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-grace\fR \fI<DUR>\fR
How long to wait after SIGTERM before SIGKILL (default 5s)
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<IDS>\fR
The jobs' IDs
.SH "JOUET CI"
Run GitHub Actions jobs on this machine
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet ci [OPTIONS] <COMMAND>
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
.SH "JOUET CI RUN"
Run a workflow's jobs here with jactionlint, and show why any failed

Runs `jactionlint run`, which needs no container engine: each job runs in a copy of the checkout, in the OS sandbox, with a throwaway home and no secrets unless given. Prints one line for the run, then each failed step with the end of its output (cut like `wait ci`'s log excerpts) and the jobs that were skipped and why; the whole transcript is kept in a log. WORKFLOW is a file of .github/workflows (the extension is optional), a path, or a workflow's `name:`. \-\-check takes a check's name as `wait ci` prints it and runs the job behind it. Arguments after `\-\-` go to `jactionlint run` as they are, such as `\-\-secret NAME=VALUE`. `\-\-secrets\-from fnox` resolves the `secrets.*` the jobs read through fnox and passes them in a temporary file, never on the command line. It shows what happens on this machine, not whether GitHub will pass: jobs for another OS are skipped. Exits 1 when a job failed, 3 when no workflow or job matches, 4 when jactionlint is missing or the run could not start.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet ci run [OPTIONS] [<WORKFLOW>] [<ARGS>] ...
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-j, \-\-job\fR \fI<ID>\fR
Run only this job and the jobs it needs. Repeatable
.TP
\fB\-\-check\fR \fI<NAME>\fR
Run the job behind this check name, such as `unit (ubuntu\-latest)`
.TP
\fB\-e, \-\-event\fR \fI<EVENT>\fR
The event that triggers the workflow (default push)
.TP
\fB\-\-log\-lines\fR \fI<N>\fR
Lines of each failed step to show (default 60; 0 shows all of it)
.TP
\fB\-\-secrets\-from\fR \fI<SOURCE>\fR
Give the jobs the secrets they read, resolved through this tool
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<WORKFLOW>\fR
The workflow. Needed when the repository has several and no \-\-check is given
.TP
\fB<ARGS>\fR
Arguments for `jactionlint run`, after `\-\-`
.SH "JOUET RULE"
List, test and trust the rules jouet's hooks run

Rules are `[[rule]]` tables in TOML files: built in, in ~/.config/jouet/rules, and in a repository's .jouet/rules once it is trusted. `jouet rule schema` prints their JSON schema; a rule with logic a matcher cannot express names a Luau `script`, written against the API `jouet rule types` prints.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet rule [OPTIONS] <COMMAND>
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
.SH "JOUET RULE LIST"
List every rule, where it comes from and whether it runs
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet rule list [OPTIONS]
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
.SH "JOUET RULE TEST"
Run a rule against a command or hook payload, or against its examples

With neither \-\-command nor \-\-payload, runs the rule's `examples`: every `fires` example must make it deny, warn or record, and every `quiet` one must leave it silent. It fails, showing what was expected and what the rule did, when one does not, or when the rule lacks either kind. \-\-all does this for every rule. \-\-replay runs the rule over every call in the hook ledger instead, and fails if it fires on none of them.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet rule test [OPTIONS] [<RULE>]
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-all\fR
Run the examples of every rule: built in, yours and this repository's
.TP
\fB\-\-command\fR \fI<CMD>\fR
A shell command to test the rule against
.TP
\fB\-\-payload\fR \fI<FILE>\fR
A Claude Code or Codex hook payload (JSON) to test against; `\-` is stdin
.TP
\fB\-\-exit\-code\fR \fI<N>\fR
The command's exit code, for a post\-tool\-use rule (default 0)
.TP
\fB\-\-replay\fR
Run the rule over the hook ledger's calls: what it fires on, and the near misses
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<RULE>\fR
A rule's name, or the path to a .toml (or .luau) rule file
.SH "JOUET RULE SCHEMA"
Print the JSON schema of rule files

Every key a `[[rule]]` table takes, with what it means. The rule files jouet writes start with a `#:schema` line, so editors that read it complete and check them.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet rule schema [OPTIONS]
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
.SH "JOUET RULE TYPES"
Print the Luau type definitions of the jouet module, for rule scripts
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet rule types [OPTIONS]
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
.SH "JOUET RULE TRUST"
Trust this repository's .jouet/rules as they are now
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet rule trust [OPTIONS]
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
.SH "JOUET RULE UNTRUST"
Stop trusting this repository's .jouet/rules
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet rule untrust [OPTIONS]
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
.SH "JOUET RULE NEW"
Write a new rule of yours, in warn mode, from a template or a winding note

Writes ~/.config/jouet/rules/NAME.toml, a rule saying why it exists, who wrote it and when, with a matcher and examples to fill in. It starts in warn mode: `jouet rule promote` makes its denials real. \-\-script adds NAME.luau for logic a matcher cannot express. With \-\-from\-note, the rule is the one `jouet wind` drafted.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet rule new [OPTIONS] <NAME>
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-from\-note\fR \fI<ID>\fR
Start from the rule a winding note drafted, such as n3
.TP
\fB\-\-why\fR \fI<TEXT>\fR
What the rule is for, in one line (the template's `why`)
.TP
\fB\-\-author\fR \fI<WHO>\fR
Who is writing it: your session's id (default: $JOUET_SESSION_ID, or the harness's)
.TP
\fB\-\-script\fR
Also write NAME.luau, a script for logic a matcher cannot express
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<NAME>\fR
The rule's name: lowercase words joined by dashes
.SH "JOUET RULE PROMOTE"
Switch a rule of yours from warn to deny

Only rules in ~/.config/jouet/rules: built\-in rules are part of jouet, and a repository's change through a reviewed PR. Logged in `jouet rule log`.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet rule promote [OPTIONS] <NAME>
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-reason\fR \fI<TEXT>\fR
Why, for the log
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<NAME>\fR
The rule's name
.SH "JOUET RULE LOG"
Show every change made to your rules, and why
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet rule log [OPTIONS] [<NAME>]
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<NAME>\fR
Only this rule's changes
.SH "JOUET RULE GAP"
Record something a rule could not express, for `jouet share \-\-idea`

jouet records on its own when a rule reads a `jouet` primitive that does not exist. This records a wish in your words: "I kept doing X; there is no primitive for Y".
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet rule gap [OPTIONS] <TEXT>
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-rule\fR \fI<NAME>\fR
The rule you were writing, if any
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<TEXT>\fR
What you wanted a rule to do, and what was missing
.SH "JOUET RUN"
Run a routine: a Luau program on jouet's primitives (also `joue`)

Routines are Luau programs, where rules are TOML configuration. `joue NAME [ARGS]` is the same as `jouet run NAME [ARGS]`. Routines are built in (land, pr\-next, ship\-check, sweep), in ~/.config/jouet/routines, and in a repository's .jouet/routines once it is trusted with \-\-trust. Each declares its arguments, so `joue NAME \-\-help` says what it takes, and the capabilities it reaches; anything else is refused (exit 4). A routine that writes acts only with \-\-yes: without it every write is previewed, and joue exits 4 (0 with \-\-dry\-run).
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet run [OPTIONS] [COMMAND]
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-list\fR
List every routine, where it comes from and what it reaches
.TP
\fB\-\-trust\fR
Trust this repository's .jouet/routines as they are now
.TP
\fB\-\-untrust\fR
Stop trusting this repository's .jouet/routines
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
.SH "JOUET RUN TEST"
Run routines' examples against their recorded responses

Each example runs the routine with its arguments against a fixture: a local fake GitHub serves its recorded responses, and jouet's commands, git, mise and the ledger answer from it too. Nothing real is reached, and file edits are only previewed. Fails (exit 1) when an example's exit code, output, JSON, previewed writes or requests differ from what it expects, or when a routine has no examples.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet run test [OPTIONS] [<ROUTINE>]
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<ROUTINE>\fR
The routine (default: every one)
.SH "JOUET RUN TYPES"
Print the Luau type definitions of the primitives
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet run types [OPTIONS]
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
.SH "JOUET WIND"
Notice costly patterns in the hook ledger and whole sessions, and propose fixes

Reads the ledger jouet's hooks keep from where it last stopped. When a command keeps failing across sessions, or agents keep sleeping instead of waiting, it drafts a rule in warn mode and keeps a note of it, which the next session start shows (two at most). The hooks run it on their own; running it by hand reads the ledger now. Off with JOUET_WIND=0.

The ledger holds only command shapes. `jouet wind digest` and `jouet wind review` read whole sessions: the user's corrections, abandoned approaches, waits and repeated exploration. A review's proposals (mise tasks, rules, routines, AGENTS.md or memory notes) are recorded with `jouet wind propose` and shown like the ledger's notes.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet wind [OPTIONS] [COMMAND]
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
.SH "JOUET WIND SHOW"
Show a note or a proposal: what was noticed, and what was drafted
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet wind show [OPTIONS] <ID>
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<ID>\fR
The id of a note (n3), a proposal (p2), a suggested review (r1) or a mise task (t1)
.SH "JOUET WIND DISMISS"
Drop a note, a proposal or a suggested review, so it is never shown again
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet wind dismiss [OPTIONS] <ID>
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<ID>\fR
The id of a note (n3), a proposal (p2), a suggested review (r1) or a mise task (t1)
.SH "JOUET WIND ADOPT"
Mark a review's proposal as applied
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet wind adopt [OPTIONS] <ID>
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<ID>\fR
The id of a note (n3), a proposal (p2), a suggested review (r1) or a mise task (t1)
.SH "JOUET WIND DIGEST"
Digest whole sessions: corrections, failures, waits, re\-reads

Reads a Claude Code or Codex transcript and reduces it to a few thousand tokens: the user's corrections and interruptions, failures and their retries, long calls, where tokens and time went (as `jouet audit` counts them), files read repeatedly, commands typed out again, and a timeline with a phase per prompt. Every item names its lines in the transcript. Secrets are redacted (with gitleaks' rules too, when a config is found) and the home directory is written as ~. Nothing leaves the machine. `jouet wind review` prints the same with a prompt for a reviewing agent.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet wind digest [OPTIONS] [<SESSION>]
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-last\fR
The most recently written session (inside a session, that session)
.TP
\fB\-\-since\fR \fI<TIME>\fR
Every session written since TIME (7d, 12h, a date or RFC 3339), the 8 most recent
.TP
\fB\-\-harness\fR \fI<HARNESS>\fR
Which transcripts to read: claude, codex or all (default all)
.TP
\fB\-\-project\fR \fI<PATH>\fR
Only sessions that ran in PATH or a directory below it
.TP
\fB\-\-budget\fR \fI<N>\fR
Tokens per session's digest, about (default 4000)
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<SESSION>\fR
A session ID, a prefix of one, or a transcript's path
.SH "JOUET WIND REVIEW"
Digest whole sessions: corrections, failures, waits, re\-reads

Reads a Claude Code or Codex transcript and reduces it to a few thousand tokens: the user's corrections and interruptions, failures and their retries, long calls, where tokens and time went (as `jouet audit` counts them), files read repeatedly, commands typed out again, and a timeline with a phase per prompt. Every item names its lines in the transcript. Secrets are redacted (with gitleaks' rules too, when a config is found) and the home directory is written as ~. Nothing leaves the machine. `jouet wind review` prints the same with a prompt for a reviewing agent.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet wind review [OPTIONS] [<SESSION>]
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-last\fR
The most recently written session (inside a session, that session)
.TP
\fB\-\-since\fR \fI<TIME>\fR
Every session written since TIME (7d, 12h, a date or RFC 3339), the 8 most recent
.TP
\fB\-\-harness\fR \fI<HARNESS>\fR
Which transcripts to read: claude, codex or all (default all)
.TP
\fB\-\-project\fR \fI<PATH>\fR
Only sessions that ran in PATH or a directory below it
.TP
\fB\-\-budget\fR \fI<N>\fR
Tokens per session's digest, about (default 4000)
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<SESSION>\fR
A session ID, a prefix of one, or a transcript's path
.SH "JOUET WIND PROPOSE"
Record what a review proposed, to show at the next session start

Reads a review's JSON reply on stdin (or from FILE): `{"session": "...", "proposals": [{"kind", "title", "why", "evidence", "scope", "draft"}]}`, as `jouet wind review` asks for. Each proposal is checked and recorded as pending: three per review and three a day at most, never one that looks like it holds a secret, never one already proposed. Recording applies nothing: the next session starts show each proposal in one line, and the agent there asks the user before applying it.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet wind propose [OPTIONS] [<FILE>]
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<FILE>\fR
Read the reply from FILE instead of stdin
.SH "JOUET UNWIND"
Propose retiring rules that are noisy or always overridden, and unused tasks

Lists your rules and a trusted repository's that the hook ledger shows are nearly always overridden, fire on a large share of calls, or no longer load. Built\-in rules are never proposed. \-\-apply moves your own rules to ~/.config/jouet/rules/retired/ and logs it; a repository's rules change through a PR. It also lists the mise tasks `jouet task apply` added that no agent session has run in the 21 days since, and still run what jouet wrote; \-\-apply removes them with `mise tasks rm`.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet unwind [OPTIONS]
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-apply\fR
Retire the proposed rules of yours, and remove the proposed tasks
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
.SH "JOUET TASK"
Propose mise tasks for commands agents keep repeating, and add one

`jouet task propose` reads this repository's recent session transcripts (Claude Code and Codex) and drafts a mise task for each command sequence, or long command, that agents ran at least 3 times in 2 sessions and no task already runs: its name, `run`, description, `depends` and `sources` where they can be inferred, how often it ran, and which file it should go in (mise.local.toml or mise.toml) with why. The hook ledger notices such sequences too, and a session start then names them. `jouet task apply ID \-\-file local|shared` adds the task through `mise tasks add`, never by editing TOML; which file is the user's choice, so ask them first. Applying needs a mise newer than 2026.10.24.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet task [OPTIONS] <COMMAND>
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
.SH "JOUET TASK PROPOSE"
Draft mise tasks for commands agents repeat in this repository

Prints every pending proposal for the repository: the task, the evidence (runs, sessions, time, output tokens), the file jouet suggests and why, and the question to ask the user. Proposals are kept with winding's notes: `jouet wind show ID` prints one and `jouet wind dismiss ID` drops it.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet task propose [OPTIONS]
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-since\fR \fI<TIME>\fR
Read the sessions written since TIME (7d, 12h, a date or RFC 3339), the 8 most recent (default 14d)
.TP
\fB\-\-harness\fR \fI<HARNESS>\fR
Which transcripts to read: claude, codex or all (default all)
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
.SH "JOUET TASK APPLY"
Add a proposed task through mise, to the file the user chose

Runs `mise tasks add \-\-json` (or `mise tasks set` for a task jouet added before) with `\-\-env local` for mise.local.toml or `\-\-path` to the repository's shared config for mise.toml, and prints the JSON mise returns. Without \-\-file it refuses (exit 4) and says which file jouet suggests: ask the user, then pass their choice. A mise older than 2026.10.24 or older is refused (exit 4). A command with shell syntax (`&&`, pipes, quotes) is written as `sh \-c '...'`, since mise quotes each word of a task's command.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet task apply [OPTIONS] <ID>
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-file\fR \fI<FILE>\fR
Which file the user chose: local (mise.local.toml, theirs, not committed) or shared (mise.toml, committed)
.TP
\fB\-\-name\fR \fI<NAME>\fR
Name the task NAME instead of the proposed name
.TP
\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR
Show what mise would write, writing nothing
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<ID>\fR
The proposal, such as t2
.SH "JOUET SHARE"
Propose a proven rule, a missing primitive, or a fix to jouet itself to its maintainers

Builds a proposal and prints the exact text, with paths, repository names, hostnames and emails replaced, and a diff of what that changed. Nothing is posted without \-\-yes, which a person must approve: it posts with your GitHub login, a bug as an Issue on jdx/jouet and anything else as a Discussion in its "Rules & ideas" category. A rule is shared only once it passes its tests, has denied for a week and fired often with few overrides. \-\-idea also covers what sessions show about jouet itself: crashes, exit codes outside its table, malformed JSON, usage errors, output agents abandon, built\-in rules overridden again and again, slow hooks, and scripts written in many sessions. At most three posts a week.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet share [OPTIONS] [<NAME>]
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-idea\fR
Share an idea: a missing primitive, or something sessions show about jouet itself
.TP
\fB\-\-list\fR
List the ideas ready to share, with their keys
.TP
\fB\-\-yes\fR
Post it; without this, only show what would be posted
.TP
\fB\-\-redact\fR \fI<WORD>\fR
Hide WORD as well, such as an internal tool's name. Repeatable
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<NAME>\fR
The rule to share; with \-\-idea, the idea's key (default: the most recorded)
.SH "JOUET AGENT-HELP"
Print a short usage guide written for agents

When to reach for each command, its key flags, the exit codes and the output modes, generated from this binary's own CLI definition.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet agent\-help [OPTIONS] [<COMMAND>] ...
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<COMMAND>\fR
A command to describe in full, such as `edit`
.SH "JOUET SKILL"
Print jouet's agent skill

A short skill that says when to reach for jouet and points at `jouet agent\-help` for the flags. `jouet setup` installs it.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet skill [OPTIONS]
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-format\fR \fI<FORMAT>\fR
claude or codex (a SKILL.md), or agents\-md (a snippet for AGENTS.md)
.TP
\fB\-\-contribute\fR
Print the optional skill for proposing rules and ideas to jouet instead
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
.SH "JOUET SETUP"
Install jouet's hooks and skill into Claude Code or Codex

Merges hooks into Claude Code's settings.json or Codex's hooks.json, keeping every other hook, and installs the skill. Running it again changes nothing; \-\-uninstall removes only what jouet added. The hooks do nothing where jouet is not on PATH.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet setup [OPTIONS] <HARNESS>
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-global\fR
Install for every project, under your home directory (the default)
.TP
\fB\-\-project\fR
Install into this repository for you alone: Claude Code's untracked .claude/settings.local.json
.TP
\fB\-\-shared\fR
Install into the repository's tracked .claude/settings.json, or .codex/hooks.json, for everyone who clones it (implies \-\-project)
.TP
\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR
Show what would change without writing anything
.TP
\fB\-\-uninstall\fR
Remove jouet's hooks and skill
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<HARNESS>\fR
claude or codex
.SH "JOUET COMPLETION"
Print a shell completion script

The script asks this binary for each completion, so it needs nothing else installed and follows whichever jouet is on PATH. Write it where your shell loads completions, or source it from your shell's startup file.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet completion [OPTIONS] <SHELL>
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-joue\fR
Complete `joue` instead, the name routines run under
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<SHELL>\fR
The shell to complete for
